    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah it might be the load on the agents. i checked the cpu/mem usage and they seem fine during the failure. i'm also thinking if it's a network thing or something with how the docker containers are spinning up. do you have any specific tools or logs i should be looking at to figure out if it's the environment causing the glitch?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If the CPU and memory look fine, then the issue is likely deeper, perhaps in the communication layer or the container lifecycle. You should definitely check the container logs *during* the failure, not just when everything is quiet, to see if the application within the container is throwing transient errors. Also, look at the Jenkins agent logs for any connection timeouts or resource allocation warnings when the test stage starts. These logs can often reveal if the container spin-up process is timing out before the test runner even gets a stable connection.",
      "source": "target",
